      "text_raw": "A transistor example of such\na shunt-shunt feedback\namplifier is shown in this\nslide. The open-loop ampli-\nfier consists of a single-tran-\nsistor amplifier followed by\na source-follower, in order\nto provide a low output\nresistance. Its value is 1/g .\nm2\nThe gain of the amplifier\nis readily found to be g r ,\nm1 o1\nwhich is again the loop gain.\nThis loop gain LG is not so\nlarge. Its value is barely 100\nand yet, all feedback rules\nstill apply.\nThe closed-loop input resistance is now R divided by the loop gain. Output resistor 1/g is\nF m2\nobviously negligible with respect to R .\nF\nThe closed-loop output resistance is then 1/g divided by the loop gain. It is now very small,\nm2\nor quasi zero.",
      "text": "A transistor example of such a shunt-shunt feedback amplifier is shown in this slide. The open-loop amplifier consists of a single-transistor amplifier followed by a source-follower, in order to provide a low output resistance. Its value is 1/g . m2 The gain of the amplifier is readily found to be g r , m1 o1 which is again the loop gain. This loop gain LG is not so large. Its value is barely 100 and yet, all feedback rules still apply. The closed-loop input resistance is now R divided by the loop gain. Output resistor 1/g is F m2 obviously negligible with respect to R . F The closed-loop output resistance is then 1/g divided by the loop gain. It is now very small, m2 or quasi zero."
